<!doctype html>
<!--
=============================================================================
  THE WEB3 FAMILY  ·  theweb3family.com
  Built with Eleventy. Shared layout lives here; page content lives in each
  page's own file under src/. Read CLAUDE.md before changing anything.
=============================================================================

  EVERYTHING IN THE OPEN SLOTS LIST IS FILLED.
  The photo is real: src/christopher.jpg, passed through by
  .eleventy.js. The phone number is real: 310-703-6003, appearing in
  the hero, the last action section, and the footer below. The footer
  also carries the real email, christopher@theweb3family.com. Every
  "Book a call with me" button on the site, including the last
  action section in src/index.njk and the footer below, shares one
  macro (src/_includes/cta.njk) and opens Calendly's real popup
  scheduler. Calendly's popup widget assets load in <head> on every
  page, since the footer's own booking button needs them everywhere
  the footer appears. The button read "Book my 15 minute call," then
  "Book my meeting with you.," then "Book a free strategy call,"
  before landing on this text; each change was a direct request, most
  recently via a Vercel toolbar comment on the header nav CTA.

  HEADER NAVIGATION
     The header used to be logo-only. Christopher asked directly for a
     hamburger menu, so the site no longer has "nothing to click but
     the phone number and the one call to action" as an absolute rule;
     treat the header nav as settled, but still don't add navigation
     links anywhere else (a page footer, inline in body copy) without
     asking first. It now holds four links: case studies, articles,
     contact, and book a meeting (the popup) — contact was added after
     the first three, once src/contact.njk existed to link to. The menu
     itself is a <details>/<summary> disclosure, the same JS-free
     pattern the case study pages already used for their weekly log,
     so it stays inside the site's zero-client-JS rule.

  ANALYTICS
     Microsoft Clarity and Google Analytics (gtag.js) are both loaded in
     <head> above, on every page. Both were an explicit request, made
     knowing each sets its own cookies (Clarity for session replay and
     heatmaps, GA4 for aggregate visit stats), a real exception to the
     "no cookies, no consent banner" rule this site was otherwise built
     around. No consent banner was added; instead, a short /privacy/
     page (src/privacy.njk) explains what's collected and why, linked
     from the footer. See CLAUDE.md section 9 for the accepted tradeoff.

  SEO
     Canonical, OpenGraph, and Twitter card tags live in <head> above,
     built from each page's own `title` and `description` front matter
     (every page has both). `og:image` points at src/og-share.jpg, a
     1200x630 static image checked into the repo, not generated at
     build time. It's a typographic card (the homepage headline, the
     wordmark, an orange rule) built from CSS and the site's own fonts,
     rendered with a one-off headless-browser screenshot rather than
     any photography or AI image generation, per CLAUDE.md's no-stock-
     photography rule. src/sitemap.njk (served at /sitemap.xml) lists
     every page from collections.all; src/robots.txt points at it.

  CASE STUDIES

     src/case-studies/ is an index page plus one full page per client,
     built from Christopher's own weekly delivery logs. All three
     clients gave written permission to be named, so every page uses
     real names, organizations, and quotes: Arthur Flatto (Love Aid
     Foundation), Howard Eley (Ask G-Pop), and Anthony Taylor (Rap
     Frogs). This replaced the earlier anonymous "#mission / #wisdom /
     #character" single-page version at /proof/, which no longer
     exists; that URL now redirects to /case-studies/ (see
     vercel.json). A fragment-specific redirect straight to each old
     anchor's new page isn't possible without JavaScript, since a URL
     fragment never reaches the server, so anyone with an old
     /proof/#anchor link lands on the index and picks the right card
     from there.

     Not every quote is published. Two quotes originally shown on the
     Ask G-Pop card turned out to be from Howard's team, not Howard,
     so they were pulled rather than misattributed. Neither that page
     nor Rap Frogs (whose client hasn't given a quote yet either)
     shows a quote section right now; the pull-quote block is simply
     left out rather than filled with a placeholder note. Don't
     attribute a quote to someone who didn't say it, and don't add a
     name anywhere without Christopher confirming that specific
     person is covered by the organization's written okay.

     Each page uses real, already-verified numbers (weeks running,
     count of delivered items, and similar) set large in rust, never
     orange: orange text fails contrast on the cream and band grounds
     these pages use. Do not invent a number to fill out a stat row.

  DESIGN NOTES, so nothing gets flattened by accident

     The design system lives in one place: src/styles.css. Every page
     shares it through this layout, so a brand-wide change (a color, a
     spacing value, the type scale) is one edit here, not one edit per
     page. That is the whole reason this site moved off a single static
     file: keep it that way. Do not duplicate styles inline in a page
     when they belong in styles.css instead.

     Pages alternate between three grounds: cream, deeper cream, and
     solid forest green. That alternation is what gives a page rhythm as
     you scroll. Do not make every section the same color.

     On desktop, most sections split into two columns: a narrow label
     column (eyebrow, heading, orange rule) on the left, body copy on the
     right. On mobile everything stacks. Paragraphs stay capped near 62
     characters even inside the wider desktop column, so the two-column
     layout still reads like a magazine spread, not a full-width sprawl.

     Buttons have sharp 3px corners and a flat offset shadow, not a soft
     drop shadow. Checkmarks are an outlined circle with a plain check
     character inside, never a filled icon.

     Two typefaces, Lora and Inter. No third. No icon sets beyond the
     outlined checkmark, no gradients, no stock photography ever, and no
     animation on scroll.

  COLOR RULES, verified by calculation

     Charcoal on cream 14.62:1. Forest on cream 12.06:1. Cream on forest
     12.06:1. Rust on cream 7.06:1. Charcoal on orange 5.08:1. Orange on
     forest 4.19:1, AA for large text only, used for the price figure and
     nothing smaller.

     Never put white text on the orange. It comes to 3.39:1 and fails.
     That is why button text is charcoal. Orange is never used for body
     text on cream either: it comes to 2.88:1. Orange is for the button,
     the rules, the card edges, and the large price figure only.

  IF YOU CHANGE THE COPY

     Read it aloud imagining an 87 year old hearing it. If a word makes him
     pause, replace it. If a sentence needs a second read, split it. No em
     dashes. First person singular, never “we”. Body text never below
     18px anywhere, including the footer and small labels.

  ADDING A NEW PAGE

     Create a new file under src/ (for example src/about.njk). Give it
     front matter with layout: base.njk, a title, and a description, then
     write the page's own sections below the front matter using the same
     rhythm as index.njk: a <section> with a top border (or class="band"
     for the alternate ground), a .wrap container, and either .split
     prose or a .cards grid. The header, footer, and hamburger menu above
     come along automatically. Do not add the new page into the
     hamburger menu itself without asking first: its four links (case
     studies, articles, contact, book a meeting) were Christopher's own
     explicit list, not a general "add every page" rule.

=============================================================================
-->
{% from "cta.njk" import button %}
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="UTF-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
<title>{{ title }}</title>
<meta name="description" content="{{ description }}">
<link rel="canonical" href="https://theweb3family.com{{ page.url | url }}">
<meta property="og:title" content="{{ title }}">
<meta property="og:description" content="{{ description }}">
<meta property="og:type" content="website">
<meta property="og:url" content="https://theweb3family.com{{ page.url | url }}">
<meta property="og:image" content="https://theweb3family.com/og-share.jpg">
<meta property="og:site_name" content="The Web3 Family">
<meta name="twitter:card" content="summary_large_image">
<link rel="icon" href="/favicon.svg" type="image/svg+xml">
<link rel="alternate icon" href="/favicon.ico">
<link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com">
<link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.gstatic.com" crossorigin>
<link href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Lora:wght@600;700&family=Inter:wght@400;500;600;700&display=swap" rel="stylesheet">
<link rel="stylesheet" href="/styles.css">
<link href="https://assets.calendly.com/assets/external/widget.css" rel="stylesheet">
<script src="https://assets.calendly.com/assets/external/widget.js" async></script>
<script type="text/javascript">
    (function(c,l,a,r,i,t,y){
        c[a]=c[a]||function(){(c[a].q=c[a].q||[]).push(arguments)};
        t=l.createElement(r);t.async=1;t.src="https://www.clarity.ms/tag/"+i;
        y=l.getElementsByTagName(r)[0];y.parentNode.insertBefore(t,y);
    })(window, document, "clarity", "script", "tnn1clsc3d");
</script>
<!-- Google tag (gtag.js), added on direct request. Loaded sitewide,
     alongside Clarity above; see the ANALYTICS note near the top of
     this file and CLAUDE.md section 9. -->
<script async src="https://www.googletagmanager.com/gtag/js?id=G-X7WWKF2SFX"></script>
<script>
  window.dataLayer = window.dataLayer || [];
  function gtag(){dataLayer.push(arguments);}
  gtag('js', new Date());
  gtag('config', 'G-X7WWKF2SFX');
</script>
{% if page.url == "/" %}
<!-- Organization + Person JSON-LD, homepage only, per a September 2026
     build instruction. This is inert structured data for crawlers, not
     executable script, so it doesn't touch the site's zero-JS rule. -->
<script type="application/ld+json">
{
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "ProfessionalService",
  "@id": "https://theweb3family.com/#organization",
  "name": "The Web3 Family",
  "description": "A one-person studio in Inglewood, California that builds websites, brand identity, pitch decks, apps, donation platforms, and fundraising infrastructure for nonprofits.",
  "disambiguatingDescription": "The Web3 Family is a nonprofit-focused design and build studio. It is not a cryptocurrency, blockchain, or Web3 software company.",
  "url": "https://theweb3family.com",
  "email": "christopher@theweb3family.com",
  "telephone": "+1-310-703-6003",
  "founder": { "@id": "https://theweb3family.com/#christopher" },
  "areaServed": "United States",
  "address": {
    "@type": "PostalAddress",
    "addressLocality": "Inglewood",
    "addressRegion": "CA",
    "addressCountry": "US"
  },
  "knowsAbout": ["nonprofit website design", "brand identity", "pitch decks", "donation platforms", "mobile apps", "fundraising strategy"]
}
</script>
<script type="application/ld+json">
{
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "Person",
  "@id": "https://theweb3family.com/#christopher",
  "name": "Christopher Clarke",
  "jobTitle": "Founder",
  "worksFor": { "@id": "https://theweb3family.com/#organization" },
  "email": "christopher@theweb3family.com",
  "telephone": "+1-310-703-6003",
  "url": "https://theweb3family.com"
}
</script>
{% endif %}
</head>
<body>

<!-- HEADER. The logo still links home. A hamburger menu (case studies,
     articles, book a meeting) sits next to it now, per Christopher's
     direct request. Built with <details>/<summary>, the same zero-JS
     disclosure pattern the case study pages already used for their
     weekly log, so it opens and closes without any client-side
     JavaScript. -->
<header>
  <div class="wrap header-row">
    <a class="brand" href="/">
      <div class="mark">CC</div>
      <div>
        <b>Christopher Clarke</b>
        <span>The Web3 Family</span>
      </div>
    </a>
    <!-- Desktop: a plain always-visible nav, per direct request
         ("can we have a better menu on desktop?"). Hidden below the
         760px breakpoint via CSS. -->
    <nav class="nav-inline" aria-label="Site">
      <a href="/case-studies/">Case studies</a>
      <a href="/articles/">Articles</a>
      <a href="/contact/">Contact</a>
      <a class="nav-cta" href="https://calendly.com/aweb3dad/15-minute-greeting" onclick="Calendly.initPopupWidget({url:'https://calendly.com/aweb3dad/15-minute-greeting'});return false;">Book a call with me</a>
    </nav>
    <!-- Mobile: the hamburger disclosure. Hidden at 760px and up, where
         .nav-inline takes over. Kept as a second copy of the same four
         links rather than trying to force a closed <details> element's
         content visible via CSS: Chromium keeps an internal collapsed
         layout state for closed <details> regardless of a `display`
         override, which was tried first and silently broke the layout
         (the nav rendered with zero width and overflowed off-screen). -->
    <details class="nav-menu">
      <summary class="nav-toggle" aria-label="Menu">
        <span></span><span></span><span></span>
      </summary>
      <nav class="nav-panel" aria-label="Site">
        <a href="/case-studies/">Case studies</a>
        <a href="/articles/">Articles</a>
        <a href="/contact/">Contact</a>
        <a class="nav-cta" href="https://calendly.com/aweb3dad/15-minute-greeting" onclick="Calendly.initPopupWidget({url:'https://calendly.com/aweb3dad/15-minute-greeting'});return false;">Book a call with me</a>
      </nav>
    </details>
  </div>
</header>

<main>
{{ content | safe }}
</main>

<footer>
  <div class="wrap footer-cols">
    <div class="footer-info">
      <p class="footer-name">Christopher Clarke</p>
      <p class="footer-line">The Web3 Family</p>
      <p class="footer-line">Inglewood, California</p>
      <p class="footer-line"><a href="tel:+13107036003">(310) 703-6003</a></p>
      <p class="footer-line"><a href="mailto:christopher@theweb3family.com">christopher@theweb3family.com</a></p>
      <p class="footer-line"><a href="/privacy/">Privacy</a></p>
    </div>
    <div class="footer-clients">
      <p class="footer-line footer-clients-label">Clients</p>
      <p class="footer-line"><a href="https://loveaid.foundation" target="_blank" rel="noopener">loveaid.foundation<span class="link-out-icon" aria-hidden="true">&#8599;</span></a></p>
      <p class="footer-line"><a href="https://askgpop.online" target="_blank" rel="noopener">askgpop.online<span class="link-out-icon" aria-hidden="true">&#8599;</span></a></p>
      <p class="footer-line"><a href="https://rapfrogs.online" target="_blank" rel="noopener">rapfrogs.online<span class="link-out-icon" aria-hidden="true">&#8599;</span></a></p>
    </div>
    {{ button("footer-cta actions") }}
  </div>
</footer>

<!--
  GoatCounter pixel, per a September 2026 build instruction: path-level
  analytics with zero JavaScript (a single hidden 1x1 <img>, no script).
  Commented out until Christopher creates the free GoatCounter account
  and supplies the real subdomain: don't invent one to fill this in, per
  the same rule that's kept a fake Splitform/analytics code off the site
  elsewhere. Once real, uncomment this on every page (move it outside
  the comment, below), and it becomes a fourth external dependency
  alongside Google Fonts, Calendly, and Clarity (section 9).

  <img src="https://MYCODE.goatcounter.com/count?p={{ page.url }}&t={{ title }}"
       alt="" aria-hidden="true" width="1" height="1"
       style="position:absolute;left:-9999px">
-->

</body>
</html>
